        "problem": "The $y$-intercept of the hyperbola $(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$",
        "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ (0-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3} & =0 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+3 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $(0,-3).$",
